    {
      "id": "H02TGBXsGqDZsdX2hRSEI",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -9359.687801563305,
      "y": 1330.1561229674262,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 275,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "Xk0Zmajh_7dyfUx8qes0h",
        "XKjMR7b9InFtilrSA8RoU",
        "yaRTm0QTrIGEZ6X_6f3Hi",
        "pahsQlQBlLNfZt7pvXOCo",
        "G8oQyv4olfHN54lPjSERR",
        "jf9QwLM-wQ1KLx7_VRxZh",
        "lkMo1B4zD3dAsvhGG83m2",
        "4D8ck-1m3t2ActiNY8GVl"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1C",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 936191535,
      "version": 8605,
      "versionNonce": 481453825,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791873362271,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Let's draw an important architectural distinction between workflows and agents:\n\n    - Workflows are systems where LLMs and tools are orchestrated through predefined code paths.\n    - Agents, on the other hand, are systems where LLMs dynamically direct their own processes and\n        tool usage, maintaining control over how they accomplish tasks.\n\n\nWhen building applications with LLMs, we recommend finding the simplest solution possible, and only\nincreasing complexity when needed. This might mean not building agentic systems at all. Agentic systems\noften trade latency and cost for better task performance, and you should consider when this tradeoff\nmakes sense.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Let's draw an important architectural distinction between workflows and agents:\n\n    - Workflows are systems where LLMs and tools are orchestrated through predefined code paths.\n    - Agents, on the other hand, are systems where LLMs dynamically direct their own processes and\n        tool usage, maintaining control over how they accomplish tasks.\n\n\nWhen building applications with LLMs, we recommend finding the simplest solution possible, and only increasing complexity when needed. This might mean not building agentic systems at all. Agentic systems often trade latency and cost for better task performance, and you should consider when this tradeoff makes sense.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": 1791802801644,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
